    "case_id": "489",
    "case_summary": "A 21-year-old right-handed student experienced a first seizure while bicycling, suddenly falling from his bike and developing generalized tonic-clonic movements lasting about 5 minutes, witnessed by a passer-by. He had no relevant medical or family history and no history of substance abuse. Notable physical characteristics included short stature (1.66 m).\n\nOn admission, he was somnolent but fully oriented. Physical examination showed enuresis. Clinical signs such as carpopedal spasm or Chvostek sign were absent.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': ['Creatine kinase: 1913 U/L (normal <170 U/L)', 'Serum calcium: 1.13 mmol/L (normal range 2.2–2.6 mmol/L)', 'Serum phosphate: 1.98 mmol/L (normal range 0.48–1.45 mmol/L)', 'TSH: normal', 'PTH: 110 ng/L (normal range 11–67 ng/L)'], 'Imaging Studies': {'Cerebral CT': {'Title': 'Head CT showing brain calcification', 'Description': 'Transverse CT scan of the head showing bilateral hyperdense areas in the basal ganglia and frontal lobes'}}, 'Other Tests': {'EEG': 'Showed dysrhythmia with diffuse slowing'}}",
